messages:
jules: We got a customer incident where retry backoff + AbortController = intermittent cancelled requests. They see ~10-30% of requests abruptly fail during retries even though backend shows the later attempt succeeded. Repro steps, logs and minimal snippet below.

jules: Node 20, undici 5.15.0, our TS SDK 1.3.2. Requests are batched client-side and we implement exponential backoff with setTimeout.

jules: Minimal repro:
```js
// BAD: single AbortController reused across retries
const controller = new AbortController();
for (let attempt=0; attempt<max; attempt++) {
  await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload), signal: controller.signal });
  // on network error -> await sleep(backoff(attempt)) then retry
}
```

jules: Observed behavior: later retry's promise gets rejected with "AbortError" and the backend still processes and returns 200 for that later attempt. Logs show the same request id on backend, but SDK got aborted.

alex: sounds like the controller from earlier attempt is being triggered when we call `clearTimeout`/`setTimeout` incorrectly? do you actually call controller.abort() anywhere?

jules: We abort in some exception handling branches and we *did* reuse the same controller so when one path aborts it cancels the other pending promise. But weirdly it only shows up when backoff delay > ~200ms and when undici is used instead of node-fetch.

evan: two notes: 1) Undici's fetch integrates with AbortSignal differently than node's polyfill — reusing a single signal across distinct request lifecycles is fragile. 2) If body is a stream, retrying requires cloning or buffering the body.

jules: Body is a string JSON, not a stream. FYI the symptom is: request A times out -> we schedule retry after backoff. Meanwhile some internal error path calls `controller.abort()` for cleanup of A and that aborts the later fetch promise B even after B was started (race).

ops-ryan: can you paste the SDK-level call pattern? are we using a global fetch wrapper that attaches same signal?

jules: Yes — our SDK had a wrapper that created one AbortController per logical operation and reused it across internal retries. Example: https://github.com/customer/repo/blob/main/lib/http.ts#L120 (private link)

integrations-bot: Related issue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/276 (retries + shared signal causes cross-attempt cancel).

alex: workaround for customers: ensure you create a fresh AbortController per attempt. i.e. move `new AbortController()` into the retry loop, and don't call abort() on controllers you expect other attempts to use. Alternatively, create independent cancellation semantics for the whole logical operation vs per-attempt timeouts.

alex: Example patch:
```js
for (let attempt=0; attempt<max; attempt++) {
  const attemptController = new AbortController();
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => attemptController.abort(), perAttemptTimeoutMs);
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, { signal: attemptController.signal, method: 'POST', body });
    clearTimeout(timeout);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('non-200');
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    clearTimeout(timeout);
    if (attempt === max-1) throw err;
    await sleep(backoff(attempt));
  }
}
```

ev an: (typo in previous message) — also check that SDK doesn't attach the outer operation signal to attempt signals by reference. You can compose signals with AbortSignal.timeout in Node 20: `const signal = AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` or use `AbortController.signal` but ensure a fresh controller for each attempt.

jules: We tried the per-attempt controller and the problem went away in customer's staging. Confirmed also that upgrading undici to 5.16.1 (emergent patch) made it less likely but the real fix is not sharing the controller.

ops-ryan: can we push a note in support KB and a quick patch in SDK? this seems like a common anti-pattern.

alex: I'll open a PR to the SDK to ensure internal retry implementation uses per-attempt controllers and to add a test that simulates aborting an earlier attempt. Link to PR when ready.

jules: also flagged billing ticket because customer saw duplicated charges due to retries — we recommended idempotency-key header and server-side idempotency handling.

evan: extra debugging tips: capture `signal.aborted` and `signal.reason` in logs, and log attempt numbers & local stack when abort happens. That helped track that an earlier cleanup path was firing later.

jules: Thanks — next steps: 1) shipping SDK patch to use per-attempt controllers, 2) KB article for customers about not reusing signals across retries + idempotency advice, 3) recommend undici upgrade if they're on older patch.

integrations-bot: NOTE: issue #276 labeled "severity:medium" and assigned to alex.

alex: I'll include the sample code + changelog entry. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
